---
const hisFields = ["Photo", "Title", "Category", "Notes for me"];
const myStudio = [
  "Orderable documents",
  "Conditional fields",
  "Image hotspots",
  "Full schema control",
];
---

<!-- Designing for the Owner -->
<section class="mb-24">
  <div class="mb-8">
    <span
      class="text-xs font-semibold text-amber-700 tracking-widest uppercase"
    >
      05 — Designing for the Owner
    </span>
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-2">
      The hardest user wasn't the customer. It was the owner.
    </h2>
  </div>

  <div class="space-y-5 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-lg max-w-3xl">
    <p>
      Before I wrote a line of schema, I asked him one question: how do you
      want to add your work? His answer set the whole direction. It had to
      work like Instagram, and it had to be in Spanish. He wasn't going to
      learn a dashboard, and he shouldn't have to.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    The upload tool, not the website, was the real problem.
  </h3>
  <div class="space-y-5 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-lg max-w-3xl">
    <p>
      I started on Decap CMS. On paper it was fine. In practice it failed the
      one device that mattered: it was clunky on mobile, and his phone was the
      only way he was ever going to post. Worse, it rebuilt the entire site on
      every single upload, slow for him and burning Netlify build credits. By
      the time I admitted it wasn't working, I'd already migrated the project
      from GitHub Pages to Netlify and moved the DNS for it. I scrapped it
      anyway and moved to Sanity. Sunk cost is still a cost, but shipping the
      wrong tool is worse.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sanity won on the thing that mattered most to him: the mobile experience.
      Drag-and-drop, image hotspots, edit-in-place. The closest thing to the
      Instagram muscle memory he already had.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    Even the right CMS was still too much for him.
  </h3>
  <div class="space-y-5 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-lg max-w-3xl">
    <p>
      On my side, I modeled the content properly: orderable gallery documents
      so he can drag his work into the order he wants, and conditional fields so
      the form stays clean. The subcategory dropdown only appears for the
      categories that actually have them (concrete and landscaping) and shows
      the right sub-options for each. That part proves I architect content
      models, not just call an API.
    </p>
    <p>
      But I got Sanity set up for him and it was still too much. Even a clean
      studio is a developer's mental model, not a contractor's. So I stopped
      handing him the CMS and built a separate page at <code
        class="font-mono text-[0.92em] text-violet-700 bg-violet-50 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded"
        >/upload</code
      >, in Spanish, that does one job. It shows him what he needs and nothing
      else:
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- His page vs my studio -->
  <div class="my-6 grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-5 max-w-3xl">
    <div
      class="rounded-2xl border border-violet-200 bg-gradient-to-br from-violet-50 to-white p-5 shadow-sm"
    >
      <p
        class="text-[10px] font-bold text-violet-700 uppercase tracking-widest mb-3"
      >
        What he sees · /upload
      </p>
      <ul class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        {
          hisFields.map((f) => (
            <li class="text-sm font-medium text-violet-800 bg-white border border-violet-200 rounded-full px-3 py-1">
              {f}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-sm">
      <p
        class="text-[10px] font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest mb-3"
      >
        What I keep · Sanity Studio
      </p>
      <ul class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        {
          myStudio.map((f) => (
            <li class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-full px-3 py-1">
              {f}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 italic max-w-3xl mb-6">
    Same data, two interfaces. The form he'll actually use, the studio I need.
  </p>

  <div class="space-y-5 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-lg max-w-3xl">
    <p>
      He fills it out like an Instagram post and submits. Behind it, <code
        class="font-mono text-[0.92em] text-violet-700 bg-violet-50 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded"
        >/api/upload</code
      > pushes the image to Sanity's CDN, creates the <code
        class="font-mono text-[0.92em] text-violet-700 bg-violet-50 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded"
        >galleryItem</code
      > document, and copies his title into the alt text so the site stays
      accessible without him ever thinking about it. The full studio is still
      there for me. He never has to see it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    The details that made it his.
  </h3>
  <div class="space-y-5 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-lg max-w-3xl">
    <p>
      That notes field is doing more than it looks. It's an async channel
      between us. He leaves a note ("put this one first," or "this is the
      Hendersons' backyard"), I see it in Sanity, I handle it. No phone call, no
      scheduling, no "are you free." The tool absorbed the back-and-forth that
      used to be a conversation.
    </p>
    <p>
      One real-world snag: he shoots on an iPhone, so every photo lands as HEIC,
      which browsers won't render. The upload flow converts it to JPEG
      server-side, so he never knows it was a problem. He just sees his work
      show up on the site.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p
    class="mt-8 max-w-3xl text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 leading-snug border-l-4 border-amber-400 pl-5"
  >
    The result is the thing I set out to build: he posts his own work, from his
    phone, in his language, and the site updates itself. He doesn't need me to
    keep his business online.
  </p>
</section>
